UPDATE companies SET description='Crossfire Technologies Inc is headquartered in St. Paul, Minnesota. The company focuses on developing chiplet interconnect solutions for AI infrastructure.

Crossfire’s product portfolio centers on three core technologies: Bridgelet, Direct Chiplet Input/Output (DCIO), and OmniDie. Bridgelets serve as an alternative to silicon interposers, providing chiplet-to-chiplet connectivity in passive or active forms compatible with industry-standard die-to-die PHYs such as UCIe, BoW, and Ultra Short Reach SerDes. DCIO streamlines the die-to-die interface by removing unnecessary ESD protection, large pads, and overpowered drivers, delivering a protocol-agnostic PHY that supports standard link layers. OmniDie enables the physical integration of multiple chiplets into a single ‘SuperChip’ that mounts directly onto a package substrate, reducing reliance on advanced packaging.

Its solutions are positioned for applications requiring disaggregated I/O, memory functions, and heterogeneous integration across product families.

Crossfire states that its Bridgelet approach eliminates the cost, complexity, and availability constraints of traditional interposers, claiming up to 50× smaller footprint, 10× lower energy per bit, and 10× lower upfront cost. DCIO is said to achieve 10× lower power, 10× lower cost, and 10× higher density compared to competing solutions.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Chiplet-to-chiplet interconnect (Bridgelet)", "Direct Chiplet Input/Output (DCIO)", "OmniDie multi-chiplet integration (SuperChip)", "Die-to-die PHY integration (UCIe, BoW, USR SerDes)", "Advanced packaging alternative for heterogeneous integration"], "industries": ["AI infrastructure", "Semiconductor", "Chiplet ecosystem"], "materials": [], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Bridgelets replace silicon interposers without their cost, complexity, or availability limits", "50\u00d7 smaller footprint, 10\u00d7 less energy per bit, 10\u00d7 lower upfront cost vs. interposers", "DCIO delivers 10\u00d7 lower power, 10\u00d7 lower cost, 10\u00d7 higher density than standard D2D PHYs", "OmniDie integrates chiplets into a SuperChip mounted directly on substrate, minimizing advanced packaging", "Compatible with UCIe, BoW, and Ultra Short Reach SerDes standards"], "facts_used": ["Headquarters: St. Paul, Minnesota -> db", "Products: Bridgelet, DCIO, OmniDie -> /", "Bridgelet features: passive/active, compatible with UCIe, BoW, USR SerDes -> /", "DCIO features: protocol-agnostic, 10x lower power/cost/density -> /", "OmniDie: SuperChip integration, mounted on package substrate -> /", "Differentiators: 50x smaller, 10x less energy/bit, 10x lower upfront cost -> /", "Target market: AI infrastructure, chiplet designers -> /"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "CrossFire\u2019s Bridgelet products provide the Chiplet to Chiplet interconnect currently provided by Interposers", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='crossfire-technologies-inc' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Crown Technical Systems was founded in 1996 in Fontana, California. It operates as part of Flex (Flextronics International), a global manufacturing and technology company. The business began as a specialist in power distribution and control solutions for utilities.

The company designs, manufactures, and integrates medium-voltage metal-clad switchgear, protective relay control panels, and custom power control enclosures. It provides complete design and fabrication services for control buildings, including polyester outdoor powder coating, dielectric floor coatings, and epoxy coatings (Carboline 890 and Maropoxy, 4–6 mils DFT). Production occurs across three North American locations, with factory assembly environments for power control assemblies (PCA), power distribution centers (PDC), and traction power substations. Switchgear compartments are bolted into extended lineups.

